jitValueTargetT -- SEQ 360 R1 / R2 on the JIT road (Tony, 2026-10-15). One testing(), no oracle: the interpreted half is
incant/pop/valueTargetT, a separate process (bear-trap #25).

R1's discriminator is tempField identity, and the jit road never hands tempField back: jitEmitUnary and jitEmitBinary
return the OPERAND, carrying the computed value as its jitValue. So the identity cannot see a computed target here,
and JVT-1 / JVT-2 / JVT-6 / JVT-8 WRITE THE OPERAND. They are pinned at that value as a KNOWN DEFECT -- a pin that moves
when the jit road learns to refuse, not a claim that the value is right. The discriminator for this road is a ruling
owed (SEQ 360 report).
  JVK-1 / JVK-2   controls
  JR2-1 .. JR2-4  R2's pins, value and kind
  JVP-1           probe, report only
